      "chunk_id": "sha256:1a0406bacf590bcfd43c8c944a87b8fbb095df3efea05098295bb5cc62991d10",
      "quote": "This is because the Random Number Generator (RNG) of the estimator is consumed (i.e. mutated) when fit is called, and this mutated RNG will be used in the subsequent calls to fit . In addition, the rng object is shared across all objects that use it, and as a consequence, these objects become somewhat inter-dependent."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "sha256:1a0406bacf590bcfd43c8c944a87b8fbb095df3efea05098295bb5cc62991d10",
      "quote": "We can see from the snippet above that repeatedly calling sgd.fit has produced different models, even if the data was the same."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "sha256:73aaf267250e56e26d414ab174b8049389d500406b432120d22948485903982e",
      "quote": "a and b will influence each other since they share the same internal RNG: calling a.fit will consume b \u2019s RNG, and calling b.fit will consume a \u2019s RNG, since they are the same."
    }
  ],
  "decision": "answer",
  "answer": "When a RandomState instance is passed to an estimator, calling fit multiple times consumes the Random Number Generator (RNG), which is then shared across objects. This causes subsequent calls to fit to use the mutated RNG, leading to inter-dependent behavior where one estimator's fit affects the other's results.",
